# docs/replay/ is BUILT OUTPUT, committed only because GitHub Pages serves main//docs and has
# no build step — the bytes have to be in the repo or the page does not exist.
#
# Store it VERBATIM. With `core.autocrlf=true` (this machine, and the Windows default) git
# would rewrite the line endings of the built JS, CSS and SVG on checkout, so the files a
# judge is served would not be the files `just replay-verify` drove a browser through. It
# would also produce a whole-bundle diff every time it is rebuilt from a machine whose
# autocrlf differs — churn that hides a real change to the replay inside noise.
#
# Scoped to this directory on purpose: the rest of the tree keeps whatever normalization it
# has today, so nothing outside the replay moves.
docs/replay/** -text

# The replay FIXTURES are hashed byte-for-byte by manifest.json, so their bytes may not depend
# on who checked them out. They were written through Python's text mode on Windows, which
# turned every `\n` into `\r\n`; the manifest recorded those CRLF hashes, git normalised the
# files to LF on the way into the index, and the pin passed for the author and failed in CI on
# the first push — an LF checkout measured against a CRLF digest.
#
# `eol=lf` makes every checkout, on every platform, produce the bytes the manifest describes.
# The capture writes LF too (`newline=""`), and test_replay_fixtures fails on a stray CR, so
# the property is enforced at all three points rather than trusted at one.
frontend/src/replay/*.json text eol=lf
